{
  "event": "PreToolUse",
  "tool_name": "Bash",
  "tool_input": {
    "command": "# Confirm the most critical bugs:
# 1. errCh race: after StreamTetragonEvents returns error, goroutine does:
#    - errCh <- err (line 42)
#    - defer close(events) fires (line 38)
#    Both happen essentially simultaneously. In the main select loop, when events is closed
#    AND errCh has data, Go selects randomly. If `case evt, ok := <-events` fires with ok=false,
#    the function returns immediately -> error message is permanently lost.

# 2. EMPTY_FORM has namespace:'default', so a new cluster-wide policy would incorrectly 
#    be created as TracingPolicyNamespaced in namespace 'default' instead of TracingPolicy.
#    User expects cluster-wide but gets namespaced. This is a functional correctness bug.

# 3. Storage shape mismatch: old localStorage has DisplayEvent without count/severity.
#    When isSameEvent matches prev[0] and increments count, NaN results.
#    Actually worse: the FIRST event loaded from storage won't have `severity`, 
#    so any dedup of it will produce { ...prev[0], count: NaN, severity: undefined }.

# 4. formToYaml round-trip: multi-binary ProcessRule [{binaries:["a","b"]}] -> 
#    YAML with values:["a","b"] -> yamlToForm -> [{binaries:["a"]},{binaries:["b"]}]
#    This loses the grouping. PolicyForm uses binaries[0] only, so only first binary per rule
#    is shown/used. When editing existing policy that had binaries:["a","b"] in one rule,
#    after round-trip both "a" and "b" are preserved (just split), which is functionally OK.
#    But the EMPTY_FORM issue is real: namespace:'default' means isNew policies are namespaced.

echo "Confirmed all major issues"",
    "description": "Confirm all identified bugs"
  }
}

