{
  "event": "PreToolUse",
  "tool_name": "Bash",
  "tool_input": {
    "command": "# Final check: EMPTY_FORM has namespace: 'default'
# But PolicyFormInput.namespace?: string (optional)
# When isNew=true, user creates a new policy with formValues=EMPTY_FORM
# formValues.namespace = 'default'
# handleSave sends { source:'form', form: formValues, action }
# formValues.namespace = 'default' → backend builds TracingPolicyNamespaced
# But user might want cluster-wide. The form Select shows 'default' selected by default
# (not 'cluster-wide'), which at least matches the value. Consistent but possibly surprising.

# More interesting: when loading an existing policy, yamlToForm returns 
# namespace: doc.metadata.namespace || undefined
# If the policy is TracingPolicy (cluster-wide), metadata.namespace is empty/undefined
# → parsed.namespace = undefined
# setFormValues(parsed) → formValues.namespace = undefined
# Select value = undefined ?? CLUSTER_WIDE = CLUSTER_WIDE — correct display
# But the EMPTY_FORM check is only a UX default issue, not a correctness bug.

# Summary of real bugs found:
# BUG 1: PolicyEditPage.tsx line 86: setPolicyMode wrapped in "if (parsed.namespace !== undefined)"
#   → cluster-wide policies (namespace=undefined) never update policyMode on YAML→Form tab switch
#   → Sigkill cluster-wide policy saved from Form tab gets wrong 'Post' action

# BUG 2: SecurityEventsProvider.tsx line 108-114: debounce cleanup on unmount clears timer
#   but does NOT call saveToStorage → last ≤2s of events lost on navigation/unmount

# BUG 3: builder.go/formToYaml.ts: if networkPorts non-empty but netAddresses empty, 
#   ports are silently dropped with no error → user intent ignored

# BUG 4: yamlToForm.ts line 84: dPort check inside the 'for sel of selectors' loop
#   searches sel.matchArgs (single selector's args only), which is correct for blacklist
#   (both DAddr and DPort in same selector) but for whitelist they're in separate selectors
#   → whitelist DPort is in selector 1, the dPort search runs on selector 1's matchArgs
#   → finds NotDPort there → works correctly. Actually not a bug.

# BUG 5: yamlToForm.ts: for whitelist+ports, networkMode could get set TWICE
#   (once from selector 0 to 'whitelist', then selector 1 might set it again if it had DAddr/NotDAddr)
#   But selector 1 only has NotDPort, so no overwrite. Not a bug.

# Real distinct bugs to report (up to 8):
# 1. PolicyEditPage.tsx:86 - namespace guard prevents policyMode update for cluster-wide policies
# 2. SecurityEventsProvider.tsx:112-114 - unmount clears pending save without flushing
# 3. builder.go:76 / formToYaml.ts:75 - ports silently dropped when netAddresses is empty
# 4. yamlToForm.ts:68-89 - networkMode set inside per-selector loop; second selector 
#    (whitelist NotDPort) has no DAddr/NotDAddr so first selector's networkMode survives
#    BUT if selectors are reordered (NotDPort first), networkMode stays 'whitelist' default
#    and addresses from NotDAddr (second selector) are still pushed — actually works
# 5. PolicyEditPage.tsx:25 - EMPTY_FORM.namespace='default' means new policies always 
#    default to namespaced even if user wants cluster-wide (minor UX, not crash)
# 6. yamlToForm.ts process parsing: each binary becomes a SEPARATE ProcessRule {binaries:[bin]}
#    vs formToYaml expects all binaries in ONE combined rule → round-trip is lossy
#    Actually looking again: allBinaries = (input.process ?? []).flatMap((r) => r.binaries)
#    So multiple ProcessRules all get merged into allBinaries → one kprobe. OK.
#    But yamlToForm pushes one ProcessRule per binary → result.process = [{binaries:['a']},{binaries:['b']}]
#    Then formToYaml flatMaps them back → ['a','b'] → correct. Round-trip preserved.

echo "summary complete"",
    "description": "Summarize all confirmed bugs"
  }
}

